# Test: an external module that augments the shared container service gets its subtree
#
# Question: when an external plugin's module augments the compiled-in
# container `service` with a child and the plugin wants root service/chrony,
# does `ze start` accept `service { chrony { ... } }` and deliver that subtree
# to the plugin?
# Why not a unit test: TestJoinAcceptsAugmentOfSharedContainer proves the
# schema holds the child. This proves the daemon parses the block and cuts the
# sub-path root for a plugin that is a separate process.
#
# The module imports ze-dns-resolver-conf, the always-compiled module that
# defines `service`, and augments /dnsresolver:service.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-6 of spec-plugin-external-yang-at-startup.
# PREVENTS: an external plugin being unable to share a container the product
#   already owns, the only sanctioned way being `augment`: today the child is
#   an unknown keyword under service.
#
# The exit assertion is of the DAEMON'S own exit. `then=stop` on the await
# makes the runner wait for daemon.ready and for the awaited line, stop the
# daemon itself, and compare the code the daemon ended with
# (docs/architecture/testing/ci-format.md, "Await", step 3). So exit 0 means
# the daemon started, ran the plugin and stopped cleanly. A plain await
# compares the peers' wait, and this file starts no peer.

tmpfs=ze.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
bus {
	endpoint acme-chrony {
		external {
			run "le test fixture plugin/external-yang augment";
		}
		encoding json;
	}
}

service {
	chrony {
		server time.example.net
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze start ze.conf:timeout=30s
await=stderr:contains=FIXTURE: external-yang generation 1 ready:then=stop
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=FIXTURE: external-yang configure root=service/chrony leaves=[service/chrony/server=time.example.net]
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
